+The problem of determining the minimum width $\min_{\|u\|=1}w(u)$, $w(u)=\max_{1\le i\le n}\langle x_i,u\rangle-\min_{1\le i\le n}\langle x_i,u\rangle$ of a point cloud $P=\{x_1,\dots,x_n\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is well known in the computational geometry literature. More specifically, one looks for two parallel hyperplanes enclosing all points with smallest distance to each other. For $d=2$ the problem can be solved exactly with runtime complexity of $\mathbf{O}(n\log{n})$ (see~\cite[\todo{26.10: find specific passage in book!}]{atallah_computational_1986}). \cite{10.1145/323233.323234} gives an algorithm for exact computation in three dimensions with runtime complexity of $\mathbf{O}(n^2)$, although algorithms exist with faster sub-quadratic expected runtime complexity, see~\cite[Corollary~3.6]{agarwal_efficient_nodate}. The main reference for this kind of problem for dimensions $d\ge4$ is~\cite{doi:10.1142/S0218195902000748}, where an exact algorithm is presented with runtime efficiency $\mathbf{O}(n^{\lceil d/2\rceil})$.
